% The Magic rewrite drops a generated rule equal to one it has kept, and must
% keep every rule that differs, however little.
%
% s/2 has a rule written twice, whose second copy is dropped, and two rules
% that differ from it only in a comparison: in its operator, or in an operand
% that is a constant rather than a variable.  Were either taken for the
% first, s(3, Y)? or s(2, Y)? would lose its answer.
%
% p/1 has forty pairs of rules whose last literals differ only in being
% negated.  Their adorned copies are indexed by a hash and found by linear
% probing, which compares every rule in a run of occupied slots, so rules of
% different kinds are compared many times over.  Were a negated literal taken
% for its positive twin, p(2)? would be false and p(X)? would answer 1 alone.

e2(1, 2).
e2(2, 2).
e2(3, 1).

s(X, Y) :- e2(X, Y), X < Y.
s(X, Y) :- e2(X, Y), X < Y.
s(X, Y) :- e2(X, Y), X > Y.
s(X, Y) :- e2(X, Y), X < 3.

e(1).
e(2).
e(3).
q(1, 0).

p(X) :- e(X), q(X, 0).
p(X) :- e(X), not q(X, 0).
